      "title": "Sentence 2 — inversion after a limiting expression",
      "text": "**Original, D:** “Only when responsibilities are clear can information reliably lead to action.”\n\n**Plain-English paraphrase:** Information can reliably produce action only if people know their responsibilities.\n\n**Structure:** Fronted “only when” clause + inverted modal and subject + main verb phrase.\n\n**Main clause:** “can information reliably lead to action.” In ordinary order: “information can reliably lead to action.”\n\n**Dependent clause:** “when responsibilities are clear,” modified by “only,” sets a necessary condition for the claimed reliability.\n\n**Important grammar:** The main clause inverts to “can information,” not “information can,” after the fronted limiting expression. The dependent clause keeps ordinary order: “responsibilities are.”\n\n**Important vocabulary:** responsibilities; reliably; lead to action.\n\n**Why it sounds academic:** It foregrounds a condition while keeping the causal claim explicit. Use this emphasis sparingly.\n\n**New IELTS-style example:** Only when public transport is dependable can commuters realistically leave their cars at home.",
