      "text": "**Original, E:** “The implication is that verification should examine how evidence was produced, as well as what the final number says.”\n\n**Plain-English paraphrase:** When checking a claim, we should inspect both the way its evidence was collected and the reported result.\n\n**Structure:** An abstract noun subject + linking verb + a content clause containing two coordinated subordinate clauses.\n\n**Main clause:** “The implication is …”\n\n**Dependent clauses:** “that verification should examine …” supplies the implication's content. “How evidence was produced” and “what the final number says” function as things to be examined. The first is an embedded interrogative clause; the second can be understood as the content expressed by the number.\n\n**Important grammar:** Embedded questions retain statement order: “how evidence was produced,” not “how was evidence produced.” The passive emphasises evidence production rather than a named researcher.\n\n**Important vocabulary:** implication; verification; as well as.\n\n**Why it sounds academic:** It links a conclusion to a concrete standard for assessing evidence, rather than merely asking for more data.\n\n**New IELTS-style example:** The lesson is that universities should examine how students were assessed, as well as what their final grades show.",
