      "text": "**Original, H:** “Reintroduction remains a possibility against which functioning systems must be prepared.”\n\n**Plain-English paraphrase:** The disease could return, so functioning systems must be ready for that possibility.\n\n**Structure:** Subject + linking verb + noun phrase modified by a defining relative clause.\n\n**Main clause:** “Reintroduction remains a possibility.”\n\n**Dependent clause:** “against which functioning systems must be prepared” modifies “possibility.” “Which” refers back to that possibility.\n\n**Important grammar:** The preposition appears before the relative pronoun: “against which.” “Must be prepared” expresses a requirement for readiness. A less formal alternative is “a possibility that systems must prepare for.”\n\n**Important vocabulary:** reintroduction; functioning systems; prepared against a risk.\n\n**Why it sounds academic:** It connects an abstract risk directly to the response it requires, using a compact relative clause.\n\n**New IELTS-style example:** Flooding remains a threat against which coastal communities must be protected.",
